  "query_preview": "..\n\nTuesday: An upper-level trough planted off the coast of British\nColumbia will eject a shortwave across the region Tuesday,\nsending a dry \"cold\" front through the Inland Northwest. With \nthe frontal passage will come breezy west winds Tuesday \nafternoon and evening through the Cascade gaps, over the \nWaterville Plateau, and into the Western Columbia Basin. Expect \nsustained speeds of 10 to 20 mph and gusts up to 30 mph. Looking\nat temperatures, the cold front won't have much of a cooling \neffect. Afternoon highs will only cool by a degree or two from \nTuesday to Wednesday, remaining in the mid to upper 80s for most\nlocations.\n\nWednesday through Friday: Wednesday will be quiet weather-wise\nwith light winds and afternoon highs in the mid to upper 80s for\nmost areas. Temperatures warm back into the upper 80s to mid 90s \nThursday and Friday as a ridge of high pressure builds up from\nthe Desert Southwest. In addition to warming temperatures,\nThursday will see another shortwave ripple through the broader\nridge, bringing another round of breezy winds across Central\nWashington with sustained speeds of 10 to 15 mph and gusts up \nto 25 mph.\n\nSaturday and Sunday: Saturday will be the big day to watch as\nthe upper level low off the coast of British Columbia tracks\ninland, sending a robust dry cold front across the Northwest and\nshifting a powerful upper-level jet directly over the region. \nThis event is looking much like what we saw this past Saturday, \nif not stronger. The latest NBM shows a 50 to 90 percent chance\nof winds gusting to 40+ mph over the Waterville Plateau and\nColumbia Basin. For the Waterville Plateau where the strongest\nwinds are expected, there is a 30 to 60 percent chance of gusts\nover 50 mph. Strong winds combined with RHs in the teens\nSaturday will result in critical fire weather conditions across\nmuch of the region. Following the cold front passage,\ntemperatures will cool by around 10 degrees with Sunday's highs\ntopping out in the low to mid 80s. /Fewkes",
